+
+    This used to open with `git add -A`, so merely EVALUATING integrity mutated
+    the repo: it staged the whole working tree, including a concurrent session's
+    in-flight files, and made `harness_lib.py integrity` unsafe for any observer
+    to run. Nothing about the answer needed the index to move — the same three
+    facts are readable directly, and the union is what `add -A` was manufacturing:
+
+      1. `diff <commit>`            commit -> WORKING TREE, for tracked paths
+                                    (catches out-of-band Bash writes and
+                                    deletions whether or not they were staged).
+      2. `diff --cached <commit>`   commit -> INDEX (catches a staged change
+                                    whose worktree copy was since reverted —
+                                    `add -A` used to overwrite that evidence).
+      3. `ls-files --others`        untracked, non-ignored paths (brand-new
+                                    files, which `add -A` used to make visible
+                                    to (1) by tracking them).
+
+    `--no-renames` is deliberate and is the one place this is STRICTER than the
+    code it replaces. git's rename detection is on by default for --name-only,
+    so a `git mv` of an out-of-scope file into an in-scope path reported ONLY
+    the destination — the source's deletion was invisible and the close passed.
+    With detection off a rename is what it physically is, a delete plus an add,
+    and both paths face the scope check. It also makes the answer independent of
+    whether the agent used `git mv` or plain `mv`, which previously gave
